.lua
book.chapter(16, "Structures and module initialization")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Group related meanings"
.pp
A structure groups values, types, and nested modules under one module name.
The syntax module Name := struct ... end creates that grouping. Qualified
paths such as Counter.bump let clients identify an operation's owner without
opening every name into the surrounding scope. Modules are especially useful
when a type and several functions jointly define an abstraction, as happens
with syntax trees, symbol tables, and compiler passes.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[module Counter := struct
  type t = { n : Int }
  val zero := { n = 0 }
  val bump c := { n = c.n + 1 }
  val get c := c.n
end
Test.equal "qualified operations" (Counter.get (Counter.bump Counter.zero)) 1
Test.equal "persistent state" (Counter.get Counter.zero) 0]=], "A small structure with a type and its operations")
.endlua
.pp
The example's type alias exposes the record representation. A client can
understand exactly what t means, but this also makes the representation part
of the public relationship between types. The next chapter shows how a
signature and opaque sealing can hide that detail. First understand the
unrestricted structure: abstraction adds a boundary to an existing grouping;
it does not turn a module into an unrelated runtime object system.
.sh 2 "Open and include have different audiences"
.pp
Open brings accessible members into the current scope for unqualified use.
Include, inside a structure, also re-exports the included members through that
structure. This distinction is about who can see the names afterwards. Open
is convenient for a local implementation; include builds a larger public
interface from an existing one. Broad use of either can make it difficult to
tell where a short name originated.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[module Arithmetic := struct
  val twice x := x + x
end
module Extended := struct
  include Arithmetic
  val four_times x := twice (twice x)
end
open Arithmetic
Test.equal "opened use" (twice 21) 42
Test.equal "reexported member" (Extended.twice 3) 6
Test.equal "extended operation" (Extended.four_times 3) 12]=],
    "Local visibility and public re-export")
.endlua
.pp
Prefer qualified paths at library boundaries and in examples where several
modules have similar operations. Map, parse, render, and get are common names
whose meanings depend strongly on their owners. A small local open can reduce
noise in a focused algorithm, but a file-wide collection of opens can make
maintenance harder. Name resolution should reveal dependencies rather than
turn them into a puzzle about declaration order.
.sh 2 "Modules are not just records"
.pp
Records hold value fields. Modules also organize type declarations, signatures,
and nested module relationships. A module alias or functor application belongs
to the modular language, not to ordinary function application on a record.
This is why a module can carry an abstract type whose identity constrains
several operations together. A record of callbacks cannot always express the
same associated-type relationship at the value level.
.pp
Nested modules provide a useful namespace hierarchy. A language tool might
have Syntax, Check, and Emit modules, each exposing values appropriate to its
stage. Keep the hierarchy meaningful rather than mirroring every source
directory mechanically. The type flow between stages is often a better guide:
syntax enters checking, checked syntax enters emission, and diagnostics retain
source information throughout.
.lua
book.graph({
    {id="S", label="Syntax module", x=0, y=0},
    {id="C", label="Check module", x=2.3, y=0},
    {id="E", label="Emit module", x=4.6, y=0}
}, {{"S", "C"}, {"C", "E"}}, "Module boundaries can follow representation boundaries")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Initialization is lazy"
.pp
Module bodies initialize on first access that forces their runtime value,
normally once per module object. This differs from ordinary eager expression
evaluation. Defining a module does not necessarily execute every value body
inside it immediately. Accessing a member can therefore be the event that
causes its initialization effects. A module whose body prints or allocates
a mutable resource has observable initialization timing.
.lua
book.algorithm("Conceptual lazy module forcing", {
    "If the module already has an initialized value, return it.",
    "Otherwise begin evaluating the module body in its captured environment.",
    "Make completed members available according to initialization progress.",
    "If a cyclic access requests an unavailable member, report a failure.",
    "On success, retain the module value for subsequent accesses."
})
.endlua
.pp
Lazy initialization can support useful dependency arrangements, but it does
not promise arbitrary cyclic initializers will succeed. If one member needs
another member that has not yet been established, the cycle still lacks a
value from which to proceed. Separate shared interfaces from effectful
initialization, and avoid using laziness as a substitute for a clear
dependency graph. The graph library later provides tools for finding cycles
and grouping mutually dependent definitions.
.sh 2 "Make initialization boring"
.pp
Pure module bodies are easier to reload, test, and embed. A body can define
functions and immutable configuration without performing an external action.
An explicit start or run function then marks the point where effects begin.
If initialization fails after an earlier external effect, a retry does not
roll that effect back. Idempotence must be designed at the application level
when repeated initialization attempts are possible.
.pp
The same principle applies to host-module installers. Installing declarations
and granting permission to call primitives are distinct operations. A module
path being recognized by the checker does not necessarily imply the associated
host operation is allowed at runtime. The embedding chapters explain this
boundary in detail. For now, distinguish source-module visibility, runtime
module initialization, and primitive capability checks as separate mechanisms.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read module parsing, checked module scopes, and lazy module values in the two
runtime files. The portable library structures provide examples of deliberate
namespace design. Build a Report module containing a pure render function
and a separate print function, then use it from two clients with qualified
paths. Add an include-based convenience module and explain which names become
part of its public interface. Review whether any initialization effect would
surprise a client merely asking for a type or member name.
